Most chat assistants stop at advice. A Task mode that actually makes the call, fills the cart or books the slot is a different kind of product, and it raises different questions about money and control. On 17 November 2026 Google announced a Task mode for the Gemini app. This post reads that announcement and Google's Gemini Apps Help release-notes page, and separates what is stated from what is missing.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. Google is a competitor of Anthropic. Stackbrook did not test Gemini or any product mentioned and has no affiliate relationship with any company mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated.*

## What Google announced

Google's post, written by Josh Woodward, says the update is rolling out starting now. Its own wording is switching the toggle from Chat to Task; "Task mode" is Stackbrook's shorthand for that. Its pitch is that Gemini can take over errands rather than just talk about them. The examples it gives include scheduling appointments, calling businesses, managing bills, shopping and checkout, and ordering groceries. You switch it on by flipping a toggle on the home screen of the Gemini app from Chat to Task.

The post says the free-of-charge rollout uses a model it calls Gemini 4 Hydrogen. It describes the audience as hundreds of millions of people, which is Google's wording and not a count we can check.

## Where it is available

The post names six countries where Task mode is rolling out free of charge: the U.S., Japan, Korea, Canada, Australia and India. Some extras are narrower. Google says a set of advanced features starts in the U.S., and it does not list which ones. Two footnotes add limits: calling through Gemini is not available in Indiana, Louisiana, Minnesota, Montana and Nebraska, and texting with Gemini needs a US phone number on an eligible mobile carrier.

| Start method | What Google says |
|---|---|
| Home-screen toggle | Switch from Chat to Task in the Gemini app |
| Text Gemini | On Android or iOS, Settings, then Channels, then Set up under Text with Gemini, then verify your phone number; starting in the U.S. |
| Email | Forward emails to a Gemini address given in the post; starting in the U.S. |

![Table-style diagram of what Google says about Task mode in the Gemini app, by area](/images/site1/gemini-task-mode-explained-what-google-says-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages.*

## Apps, payments and receipts

Google says Task mode connects to more than 60 popular apps and that developers can apply to a Connected Apps Platform. For purchases, the post says payments use the Agent Payments Protocol, known as AP2, with Mastercard and Visa, and lists American Express as coming soon. It says you can set a hard spending cap upfront, but it does not say what amount you can choose or whether there is a ceiling. Receipts and tracking go to Google Wallet, which the post says is expanding to iOS starting today. A Universal Commerce Protocol integration is described as up next, so it is a plan and not a feature today.

## Safety controls Google describes

According to the post, tasks run in a hardware-isolated virtual machine on Google Cloud's Agent Compute Engine that is dedicated to your account. The post says Gemini is designed to ask for your confirmation or pre-approval before clicking Buy, and you can set routine actions to Always allow. You can disconnect apps, adjust task settings or delete chat history. These are Google's own descriptions of its design; we have not seen independent testing of how well they work in practice, and Stackbrook has not tried them.

## What the pages do not say

This is the part worth reading twice. The announcement does not give usage or task limits, a later price or paid tier, or an end date for the free-of-charge rollout. It does not give the spending-cap amount or an American Express date. It also does not say how Task mode relates to Gemini Spark, the scheduled-tasks and skills feature described on Google's help page.

That help page is titled What's new for Gemini Spark, and as we read it on 24 November 2026 its newest entry was dated 17 July 2026. It does not mention the 17 November announcement or use the name Task mode. It does show that Spark started rolling out on 19 May 2026 in the United States with Schedules and Skills, and that later entries covered Mac availability, topic monitoring and smarter notifications. Whether Task mode is the same product, a successor or a separate feature is unconfirmed, so treat any link between them as unconfirmed.

## Questions to ask before handing over errands

This is general information, not financial or legal advice. If Task mode reaches your account, Google's own description suggests a few questions. Do you want to set a spending cap before the first purchase? Do you want Always allow on any action that moves money? Which of the 60-plus connected apps hold accounts you would not want automated? Stackbrook's own suggestion, not Google guidance, is to start with low-stakes chores and read each confirmation prompt properly before approving it.

## Bottom line

Google presents Task mode as a free-of-charge way to let Gemini run errands, with an isolated environment and, by design, confirmation before buying. The numbers that matter most for planning, such as limits and any future price, are not in the post. Until Google publishes them, or independent testers do, the sensible reading is a promising but thinly specified launch.
